117:
This article has been enacted for the purpose of:
(1) Promoting the health and general welfare of the city;
(2) Establishing reasonable and ascertainable standards for the regulation and control of the licensing and sale of alcoholic beverages;
(3) Protecting and preserving schools and churches giving to the character of the area and their peculiar suitability for particular uses, the congestion in roads and streets, and with a general view of promoting desirable living conditions;
(4) Sustaining the stability of neighborhoods and property values;
(5) Protecting against the evils of concentration of retail licenses for alcoholic beverages in one family and/or corporation, and preventing an undesirable person from engaging or having an interest in alcoholic beverages or the sale thereof in the city;
(6) Considering the proximity to the proposed location of other establishments selling alcoholic beverages;
(7) Considering adequate parking availability; and
(8) Considering the feelings and attitudes of citizens residing in the area adjacent to the proposed location.
(Ord. of 4-18-1991, § 2)

129:
The mayor and council, in passing upon an initial application for an alcoholic beverage license at the final meeting thereon, shall be guided by the following factors as to whether to grant or to deny such application:
(1) The proximity of the proposed location to any other establishment selling alcoholic beverages of any nature;
(2) The character of the neighborhood immediately adjacent to the proposed location;
(3) The proximity of churches, schools, and playgrounds to the proposed location;
(4) Whether the proposed location has adequate off-street parking facilities or other parking available for its patrons;
(5) Whether the location would tend to increase and promote traffic congestion and resulting hazards therefrom;
(6) The feelings and attitudes of those residing in the area adjacent to the proposed location;
(7) Whether any malt or alcoholic beverage license previously issued for such location was ever revoked for cause by the city council;
(8) Whether the applicant has ever sold alcoholic beverages illegally in the municipality;
(9) The criminal record, if any, of the applicant and the outlet manager, their general good character and reputation, and their financial responsibility; provided, that nonpayment of federal, state, county or city taxes shall be prima facie evidence of lack of financial responsibility;
(10) The information required in the application or statement and whether all requirements as to notice and advertisements have been complied with; and
(11) The history or reputation of the building or establishment proposed for the sale of alcoholic beverages for fighting, shooting, stabbing, or other acts of violence; gambling; illegal dealing in the alcoholic beverages or drugs; or other violations of the law.
(Ord. of 4-18-1991, § 14)
State Law reference— Location restrictions regarding sale of alcoholic beverages, O.C.G.A. § 3-3-21; authority of municipalities to regulate and determine business locations, O.C.G.A. § 3-4-49.

